WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.020 --> 00:00:04.038
From the high street to haute couture,

00:00:04.038 --> 00:00:07.790
fashion is a 1.3 trillion dollar industry,

00:00:07.790 --> 00:00:10.743
but it comes with a hefty
environmental price tag.

00:00:11.960 --> 00:00:13.760
Cotton production is being blamed

00:00:13.760 --> 00:00:16.120
for depleting water
sources and contaminating

00:00:16.120 --> 00:00:18.063
the environment with pesticides.

00:00:19.020 --> 00:00:21.070
Chemical waste from clothes manufacturing

00:00:21.070 --> 00:00:23.370
has devastated rivers in Asia,

00:00:23.370 --> 00:00:25.910
and some estimates suggest
the fashion industry

00:00:25.910 --> 00:00:28.710
is on course to create 1/4 of projected

00:00:28.710 --> 00:00:30.850
global carbon emissions by 2050.

00:00:30.850 --> 00:00:33.090
If you take design, a lot of it is

00:00:33.090 --> 00:00:35.207
take, make, dispose.

00:00:35.207 --> 00:00:36.830
There's another looming threat

00:00:36.830 --> 00:00:38.220
for the fashion industry,

00:00:38.220 --> 00:00:39.681
and it's going to put clothing materials

00:00:39.681 --> 00:00:42.183
under the microscope like never before.

00:00:53.400 --> 00:00:58.110
This man is known as the godfather
of microplastic research.

00:00:58.110 --> 00:01:01.563
In fact, Professor Richard
Thompson invented the term.

00:01:02.822 --> 00:01:05.910
Microplastics are basically
just small fragments

00:01:05.910 --> 00:01:08.943
of plastic that are
accumulating in the environment.

00:01:09.900 --> 00:01:11.691
His latest research
has uncovered something

00:01:11.691 --> 00:01:14.913
that has sent shock waves
around the fashion industry.

00:01:17.080 --> 00:01:19.210
The data we've collected show

00:01:19.210 --> 00:01:21.590
significant increase in the
quantities of microplastic

00:01:21.590 --> 00:01:22.520
in the environment,

00:01:22.520 --> 00:01:25.397
and a lot of what we found in
that study was plastic fibers.

00:01:30.551 --> 00:01:32.850
Synthetic materials like polyester,

00:01:32.850 --> 00:01:36.200
nylon, and acrylic are
made of plastic fibers.

00:01:36.200 --> 00:01:39.303
These degrade and break up
in a washing machine cycle.

00:01:40.870 --> 00:01:42.784
If we take a domestic washing load,

00:01:42.784 --> 00:01:47.000
that could release up to
700,000 fibers in a single wash.

00:01:47.000 --> 00:01:48.940
Now that's gonna go to
waste water treatment.

00:01:48.940 --> 00:01:51.070
Some of those fibers will be intercepted

00:01:51.070 --> 00:01:52.290
in waste water treatment,

00:01:52.290 --> 00:01:53.520
but a good number will potentially

00:01:53.520 --> 00:01:55.560
escape to the environment.

00:01:55.560 --> 00:01:58.460
The data suggests that they
are accumulating year on year.

00:01:59.740 --> 00:02:02.760
Between 2000 and 2016,

00:02:02.760 --> 00:02:05.530
the use of polyester by
the global garment industry

00:02:05.530 --> 00:02:10.530
increased from 8.3 to 21.3
million tons annually.

00:02:11.950 --> 00:02:13.920
1/3 of the fish that I
collect from a sample

00:02:13.920 --> 00:02:15.199
in the English Channel near to here,

00:02:15.199 --> 00:02:17.600
have got synthetic pieces in their guts.

00:02:17.600 --> 00:02:21.370
10, 20, 30 years time, the
quantities in those organisms

00:02:21.370 --> 00:02:23.083
is only going be greater.

00:02:23.083 --> 00:02:25.720
The long term effects of these plastics

00:02:25.720 --> 00:02:29.860
in the food chain, and even
in humans, are still unknown.

00:02:29.860 --> 00:02:32.865
But Professor Thompson's
discovery is ringing alarm bells.

00:02:32.865 --> 00:02:35.399
When I talk to designers,
they tell me that

00:02:35.399 --> 00:02:38.850
shedding of fibers, and indeed,
end of life recyclability

00:02:38.850 --> 00:02:41.660
was never part of the design brief.

00:02:41.660 --> 00:02:44.240
Environmental considerations like these

00:02:44.240 --> 00:02:47.723
are making some fashion brands
go back to the drawing board.

00:02:53.050 --> 00:02:55.733
Tom Kay has spent his
life around the ocean.

00:02:56.740 --> 00:02:59.037
In 2003, he launched a brand that creates

00:02:59.037 --> 00:03:01.430
functional and sustainable products

00:03:01.430 --> 00:03:03.463
for those that share a love of the sea.

00:03:05.100 --> 00:03:08.309
His company is experimenting
with different design processes

00:03:08.309 --> 00:03:11.633
in order to lessen it's
environmental impact.

00:03:13.160 --> 00:03:14.840
If you take design, a
lot of it at the minute,

00:03:14.840 --> 00:03:17.330
is take, make, dispose.

00:03:21.775 --> 00:03:24.850
That is totally unsustainable.

00:03:24.850 --> 00:03:26.760
We really have to address
the root of the problem,

00:03:26.760 --> 00:03:31.460
and that's redesigning out
harmful fibers, harmful fabrics,

00:03:31.460 --> 00:03:32.773
harmful processes.

00:03:33.840 --> 00:03:36.910
The company uses only organic cotton.

00:03:36.910 --> 00:03:40.484
It's developed a recycled
polyester insulation for jackets,

00:03:40.484 --> 00:03:44.790
and has created its very own
wetsuit recycling program.

00:03:44.790 --> 00:03:46.360
Polymer, polyester, you can take it back

00:03:46.360 --> 00:03:49.060
and recycle it time, time and time again.

00:03:49.060 --> 00:03:51.184
Finisterre's design team has also returned

00:03:51.184 --> 00:03:54.117
to one of the oldest
materials known to man.

00:03:56.910 --> 00:03:58.073
This is really nice.

00:03:58.073 --> 00:03:59.920
[Deep Female Voice] I actually
really love it in the navy.

00:03:59.920 --> 00:04:02.094
We've been big fans of
wool since we started.

00:04:02.094 --> 00:04:03.440
It's a great fiber.

00:04:03.440 --> 00:04:04.380
It's a natural fiber.

00:04:04.380 --> 00:04:07.360
It's biodegradable and
it's fully traceable.

00:04:07.360 --> 00:04:09.739
Finisterre is one of 2,700

00:04:09.739 --> 00:04:12.390
B Corp approved Companies.

00:04:12.390 --> 00:04:14.650
Members, spanning many industries,

00:04:14.650 --> 00:04:17.100
are assessed on their
environmental performance

00:04:17.100 --> 00:04:19.040
and have a legally binding commitment

00:04:19.040 --> 00:04:21.695
to put sustainability before profit.

00:04:21.695 --> 00:04:23.180
It isn't a done deal,

00:04:23.180 --> 00:04:24.240
we want fifty years to deliver this.

00:04:24.240 --> 00:04:26.310
It's the thing we work at every day

00:04:26.310 --> 00:04:28.210
to kinda improve on and get better at.

00:04:32.840 --> 00:04:34.490
One designer is turning fashions

00:04:34.490 --> 00:04:36.193
plastic problem on it's head.

00:04:37.670 --> 00:04:40.349
Javier Goyeneche runs ECOALF.

00:04:40.349 --> 00:04:43.060
He's taking plastics out of the ocean

00:04:43.060 --> 00:04:44.460
and turning them into shoes.

00:04:45.510 --> 00:04:46.863
The sneakers is the result

00:04:46.863 --> 00:04:48.916
of more than two years of R&amp;D.

00:04:48.916 --> 00:04:51.781
They're active, urban, comfortable.

00:04:56.860 --> 00:04:59.569
All the upper is made
from 100% plastic bottles

00:04:59.569 --> 00:05:01.210
from the bottom of the ocean.

00:05:01.210 --> 00:05:03.357
The outsole is made from algae.

00:05:03.357 --> 00:05:04.820
And his shoes rarely

00:05:04.820 --> 00:05:06.330
go through a washing cycle.

00:05:06.330 --> 00:05:08.790
He's limiting the plastic microfibers

00:05:08.790 --> 00:05:10.063
that end up in the ocean.

00:05:10.998 --> 00:05:13.148
ECOALF is creating high end fashion

00:05:13.148 --> 00:05:15.870
from household and industrial waste

00:05:15.870 --> 00:05:19.083
sourced from fisherman in the
Mediterranean and Thailand.

00:05:20.630 --> 00:05:22.630
We started working with three fishermen

00:05:22.630 --> 00:05:24.870
off the east coast of Spain
and now we have nearly

00:05:24.870 --> 00:05:27.840
3,000 fishermen taking waste
out of the ocean every day.

00:05:27.840 --> 00:05:30.570
We've taken already 250 tons,
which we then convert into

00:05:30.570 --> 00:05:33.240
polymer yarn, fabric, and products.

00:05:33.240 --> 00:05:34.611
But Javier's also pioneering

00:05:34.611 --> 00:05:38.940
a new synthetic material
made from recycled plastic

00:05:38.940 --> 00:05:41.080
that doesn't shed fibers in the same way

00:05:41.080 --> 00:05:42.463
as current materials.

00:05:43.620 --> 00:05:45.610
We started a project one year ago,

00:05:45.610 --> 00:05:48.010
which is with the yarn we're
getting from the ocean,

00:05:48.010 --> 00:05:49.826
we want to start investing in,

00:05:49.826 --> 00:05:52.070
how can we create a
yarn that doesn't throw

00:05:52.070 --> 00:05:55.010
microfilament to the system again?

00:05:55.010 --> 00:05:56.830
Javier is one of a growing number

00:05:56.830 --> 00:06:00.540
of boutique brands
using recycled plastics.

00:06:00.540 --> 00:06:02.410
The way we do things are
much more complicated,

00:06:02.410 --> 00:06:03.640
not only much more expensive,

00:06:03.640 --> 00:06:04.473
and at the end of the day,

00:06:04.473 --> 00:06:07.940
it's much easier to go a
fabric show, buy a fabric,

00:06:07.940 --> 00:06:09.972
produce a garment, and sell it.

00:06:09.972 --> 00:06:12.100
The sooner bigger fashion labels

00:06:12.100 --> 00:06:13.600
and brands follow suit,

00:06:13.600 --> 00:06:15.710
the cheaper this environmentally friendly

00:06:15.710 --> 00:06:16.760
clothing will become.

00:06:22.317 --> 00:06:25.526
There are few laws regulating
the use of synthetic materials

00:06:25.526 --> 00:06:27.684
in the fashion industry.

00:06:27.684 --> 00:06:30.210
Of course I'm concerned about
the natural environment.

00:06:30.210 --> 00:06:34.440
I'm a marine biologist that
works in marine habitats.

00:06:34.440 --> 00:06:36.320
Microplastics are consistent and we know

00:06:36.320 --> 00:06:37.193
that they're accumulating

00:06:37.193 --> 00:06:39.160
and we know that we're finding marine life

00:06:39.160 --> 00:06:41.695
encountering them on a regular basis.

00:06:41.695 --> 00:06:44.760
More research is needed
into the potential harm

00:06:44.760 --> 00:06:45.983
caused by microplastics.

00:06:47.090 --> 00:06:49.500
But their very presence
in the natural environment

00:06:49.500 --> 00:06:52.700
poses serious concerns and challenges

00:06:52.700 --> 00:06:54.738
for the fashion industry.

00:06:54.738 --> 00:06:58.440
Can businesses accused of
putting style over substance

00:06:58.440 --> 00:07:00.777
get to grip with the materials their using

00:07:00.777 --> 00:07:03.823
and impact they may be
having on the planet.

